{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head a668051e39ce7060ef96cfb02d83b2a20c701b0d, artifact ca38797294c5ac307b85b45b40c87e20834032dc.\n\nTwo of the three conditions are met and the third is not, deliberately. The reason is recorded as assert 8729599db37d5c129dc94aec8d5abc2fff5f570b and summarised here.\n\nSatisfied is terminal. Reports are settled before retirement, so retiring a request whose report was already ratified no longer overwrites the outcome. Reproduced against this workroom first: five commitments were showing an acknowledged completion as cancelled. My first probe for that returned zero, because it read the report field — which is exactly the field the defect discards — and I only found the five by going through provenance instead. The bug was measuring itself.\n\nThird-party retirement has its own label. Retirement now carries the actors holding it, and a withdrawal by anyone other than the author reads revoked rather than reneged or cancelled. This shape does not occur in the live log: all four reneged rows were promises retired by their own maker, so I built it in tests rather than claim a sighting I do not have.\n\nNot met: stop naming a waited-on performer in withdrawn and stale rows. I implemented it, three tests failed, and the tests were right. TestStaleCommitmentKeepsItsWaitingParty pins the opposite for a stated reason — staleness is a qualifier, not a discharge, and a promise whose basis moved is still owed — established after this request was filed, on evidence about what flattening those rows had hidden. Clearing the waited-on party would make the row read as though nobody owed anything. The withdrawn half of that condition describes a case that does not exist: a request retired before anyone promised has no performer to name.\n\nSix terminal states now have tests where none had any. Both fixes are mutation-checked: ignoring the superseding actor reproduces the blame misattribution on three subtests, and restoring the old ordering reproduces the erased satisfaction on the golden and on the new test.\n\nThe new word is threaded through every consumer — query filter, terminal lane, MCP enum and its reference page, browser closed statuses, colour and label. Two of those I missed on the first pass and the gates caught: the docset surface gate found the undocumented enum value, and the lane-table test found its own hand-maintained mirror of what the fold emits.\n\nThe pinned legacy projection moves by three lines. That guard says to update it only through an explicit migration review, which is what this is.\n\ngo vet, the full Go suite, tsc and the browser tests pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ca38797294c5ac307b85b45b40c87e20834032dc","branch":"request/commitment-honesty","head":"a668051e39ce7060ef96cfb02d83b2a20c701b0d","status":"ready-for-review"}}